{"title":"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_November_2025)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, November 2025)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine, with proof, whether or not one can find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/02406980c13ebb300d7781a2f713a3c6ba220b81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1975$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> points on the circumference of a circle with unit radius such that the distance between any two of them is a rational number.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since there are infinitely many primitive Pythagorean triples, there are infinitely many angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> s.t. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f833fd2cf5e2a698679032fb0c6d70b21be68c87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin\\theta, \\cos\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" /> are both rational. Call such angles good. By angle-sum formulas, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> are good, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/c/3dcd297a0408131968aabf934037fdb7a817d63a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b,a-b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" /> are also good.\n</p><p>For points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/6/5f642cbbd390b4882363ab6b5c2906dbf6be6cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> on the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/0/9607ad640db694f615fa60e86cfe5f911116f0a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AB$\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> be the angle subtended by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Now inductively construct points on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> s.t. all angles formed by them are good; for 1,2 take any good angle. If there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points chosen, pick a good angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and a marked point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> s.t. the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f39e488f0c92442548630fa20ac723e0a9a053.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AB=\\theta$\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" /> is distinct from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points. Since there are infinitely many good angles but finitely many marked points, such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> exists. For a previously marked point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/b/9fb49721701497ea7cbf7faf7a84622044e634de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BP=\\pm \\angle AP\\pm \\angle AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"183\" height=\"13\" /> for suitable choices for the two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/91205e81c98c486418902c88d5c396f5355491f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/6/d86975e49b52e12b2475dd8bfb3c8247b6ea4f10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AP ,\\angle AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" /> are both good, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d3883edc10c0c257197d19abaf033a0191a9db8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BP$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> is good, which finishes induction by adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Observe that these points for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/0/0105a7f64e63fef1f0d1b619137217bda18503f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1975$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" /> work: since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/f/16f331531f4fb856ae775f1fdab446e6afc094a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=2\\sin\\angle AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"133\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/6/5f642cbbd390b4882363ab6b5c2906dbf6be6cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> on the circle, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is rational, and so we're done.\n</p><p>The above solution was posted and copyrighted by tobash_co.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_November_2025.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_November_2025)\">Remarks (added by pf02, November 2025)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, November 2025)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The solution given above is written in a very messy way.  It is\nessentially correct (if we disregard some minor errors), and it\nis not too hard for a diligent reader to make sense of it.\n</p><p>I will give below another solution, very similar to the solution\ngiven by kunterbunt on the discussion page\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h61051\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h61051</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The answer is YES: we can find 1975 points on the circle such that\nthe distance between any two of them is a rational number.  In fact,\nwe can find infinitely many such points on the unit circle.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/0/580ed714d1553bf5ba58b71cd51d2d813313aaeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Proof}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" />:  Start with two diametrically opposed points\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/f/bef747a48a2a8d43c4841e12c89f2de2502a672e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" />.  Corresponding to any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_triple\" title=\"Pythagorean triple\">Pythagorean triple</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef02dac342fced80407fbebb00514b2c931ef59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m, n, p)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />\ntake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/6/38662e4bc185b066402f3085c7553e7b12f832b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = \\frac{2m}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"83\" height=\"42\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/2/1c2e0776c440b0bbf482ed1ecf2686b16fd56d72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = \\frac{2n}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"79\" height=\"42\" />.\nThe triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is a right triangle, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is on\nthe circle.  We know that there are infinitely many such points\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.  Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/8/e48ffa92d6dedfc493fcb24c80161ab131195671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA, CB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> are rational.\n</p><p>Now, we want to prove that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> are two such points, then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is rational.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are on opposite sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />,\nthen using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ptolemy%27s_theorem\" title=\"Ptolemy&#39;s theorem\">Ptolemy's theorem</a> (see also\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy&#39;s_theorem\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy&#39;s_theorem</a>) we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/c/c6c01e930a26ee89e2f5b14ad4c84e59bdb1653a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 XY = XY \\cdot AB = AX \\cdot YB + AY \\cdot XB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"330\" height=\"16\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is\nrational.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> are on the same side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, then\n(assuming <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is closest to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />)\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed2a63acf9ad7880d36e04bac0b1e91bccec8482.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AY \\cdot XB = XY \\cdot AB + AX \\cdot BY = 2 XY + AX \\cdot BY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"425\" height=\"16\" />,\nso again, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is rational.\n</p><p>This finishes the proof.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/b/84bff2984843b952549820728fdb1f03275c4e0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Note}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />:  We don't really need to invoke Ptolemy's\ntheorem.  Instead, we could use the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/b/6fb300dacfcde1b57ad070409262b1743e0cd966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5e108fdc4bf4fb6151cb922992f23bc6c35985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos$\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" /> of\nthe various angles involved, and use well known trigonometric\nidentities to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is rational.\n</p><p>For example, in the case when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> are on opposite sides\nof the diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is rational iff\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa87d69d450921983963f7d8634790c87a8423ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\angle XAY)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"96\" height=\"20\" /> is rational (because of the law of sines).\nFurthermore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d6bfa82ba43b383b59c4329459d88e6d200ac49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle XAY = \\angle XAB + \\angle BAY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"219\" height=\"16\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/c/42c39257d2cea74a9ae55bd548de58e24e6b8d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\angle XAY) = \\sin(\\angle XAB) \\cos(\\angle BAY) + \\sin(\\angle BAY) \\cos(\\angle XAB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"525\" height=\"20\" />.\nAll the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/b/6fb300dacfcde1b57ad070409262b1743e0cd966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5e108fdc4bf4fb6151cb922992f23bc6c35985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos$\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" /> in the right hand side are rational,\nsince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/2/ab2dffa181f74f75b3e851dbaca43a641abda520.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XA, XB, YA, YB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" /> are rational because of the ways we\nchose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is a diameter.\n</p><p>The case when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> are on the same side of the diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> \nis very similar.\n</p><p><br />\nThe original thread for this problem can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p2603158\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO\" title=\"1975 IMO\">1975 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017172029\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.034 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.052 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 301/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 895/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 12211/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.843      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.843      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Sa